UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 26, Lesson 2: Singing songs together', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"When a group sings together, they can sing in unison, which means everyone sings the same notes at the same time. In a round, such as ''Row, row, row your boat'', the group splits and each part begins at a different time while singing the same tune. A song may have verses that tell the story and a chorus that repeats. To sing together well, listen to the other parts, keep the beat and watch the leader. Count in before starting, and sing at a volume so you can hear the other voices.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nIn a round, a singer gets confused by the other group''s tune. Write two ways they can keep their place.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Listen to my own group and sing a little softer than the others, and look at the leader''s beat. I can also follow the words on a lyric card.'' Staying with your own part and watching the leader helps hold your place.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving that singing in a round means you should sing louder than the other group to be heard. Each group sings at the same volume so you can hear both parts.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nLearn a short round with a family member. Write down the words, mark where the second group begins and sing it as a round. Then sing in unison. Write which was harder and one thing that helped you keep your part.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"When a group sings together, they can sing in unison, which means everyone sings the same notes at the same time. In a round, such as ''Row, row, row your boat'', the group splits and each part begins at a different time while singing the same tune. A song may have verses that tell the story and a chorus that repeats. To sing together well, listen to the other parts, keep the beat and watch the leader. Count in before starting, and sing at a volume so you can hear the other voices.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Create and perform","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-MUS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Learn a short round with a family member. Write down the words, mark where the second group begins and sing it as a round. Then sing in unison. Write which was harder and one thing that helped you keep your part. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using unison on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Believing that singing in a round means you should sing louder than the other group to be heard.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Listen to my own group and sing a little softer than the others, and look at the leader''s beat.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing that singing in a round means you should sing louder than the other group to be heard. Each group sings at the same volume so you can hear both parts. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating unison on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with unison, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about unison with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for unison. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for unison. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson.","''Listen to my own group and sing a little softer than the others, and look at the leader''s beat.","Believing that singing in a round means you should sing louder than the other group to be heard.","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material."],"question":"In a round, a singer gets confused by the other group''s tune. Write two ways they can keep their place. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for unison; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of a round and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Believing that singing in a round means you should sing louder than the other group to be heard.","When a group sings together, they can sing in unison, which means everyone sings the same notes at the same time.","''Listen to my own group and sing a little softer than the others, and look at the leader''s beat."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with unison?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named a round and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Believing that singing in a round means you should sing louder than the other group to be heard.","Name a round and explain why it matters only, without linking it to unison.","Apply unison to new material and name the a round and explain why it matters used."],"question":"Which response best applies unison with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on unison. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing that singing in a round means you should sing louder than the other group to be heard.","''Listen to my own group and sing a little softer than the others, and look at the leader''s beat.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance."],"question":"Which response best uses elements, techniques and audience effect for unison?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for unison.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Believing that singing in a round means you should sing louder than the other group to be heard."],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about unison?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Example: a class sings ''Row, row, row your boat'' in unison first.","Believing that singing in a round means you should sing louder than the other group to be heard.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — When a group sings together, they can sing in unison, which means everyone sings the same notes at — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue."],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for unison?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing that singing in a round means you should sing louder than the other group to be heard.","Name a round and explain why it matters only, with no linked example.","Learn a short round with a family member.","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which independent response best applies unison to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about unison with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating unison on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using unison on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for unison. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with unison, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Believing that singing in a round means you should sing louder than the other group to be heard.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Listen to my own group and sing a little softer than the others, and look at the leader''s beat.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w26-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise unison.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Music","success_criteria":["I can use unison with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a round and explain why it matters.","I can practise a verse and chorus independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a part needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"When a group sings together, they can sing in unison, which means everyone sings the same notes at the same time.","options":["When a group sings together, they can sing in unison, which means everyone sings the same notes at the same time.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about unison with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a class sings ''Row, row, row your boat'' in unison first. Then the leader splits them into two groups. Group 1 starts, and Group 2 begins when Group 1 reaches ''gently down the stream''. Each group keeps its own beat and listens to the other. At the end, Group 1 finishes first and Group 2 finishes last, so the leader signals the end together.\n\nTRY FIRST\nIn a round, a singer gets confused by the other group''s tune. Write two ways they can keep their place.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Listen to my own group and sing a little softer than the others, and look at the leader''s beat. I can also follow the words on a lyric card.'' Staying with your own part and watching the leader helps hold your place.","explain":"When a group sings together, they can sing in unison, which means everyone sings the same notes at the same time. In a round, such as ''Row, row, row your boat'', the group splits and each part begins at a different time while singing the same tune. A song may have verses that tell the story and a chorus that repeats. To sing together well, listen to the other parts, keep the beat and watch the leader. Count in before starting, and sing at a volume so you can hear the other voices.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing that singing in a round means you should sing louder than the other group to be heard. Each group sings at the same volume so you can hear both parts. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Unison and a round.","caption":"Same tune, different starts.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Unison</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">everyone sings the same</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Round</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">parts start one after another</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: a class sings ''Row, row, row your boat'' in unison first. Then the leader splits them into two groups. Group 1 s Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: a class sings ''Row, row, row your boat'' in unison first. Then the leader splits them into two groups. Group 1 s Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about unison with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a class sings ''Row, row, row your boat'' in unison first. Then the leader splits them into two groups. Group 1 starts, and Group 2 begins when Group 1 reaches ''gently down the stream''. Each group keeps its own beat and listens to the other. At the end, Group 1 finishes first and Group 2 finishes last, so the leader signals the end together. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Verse and chorus.","caption":"The chorus repeats.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Verse</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">tells the story</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Chorus</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">repeats each time</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about unison with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving that singing in a round means you should sing louder than the other group to be heard. Each group sings at the same volume so you can hear both parts. Here is a more helpful example: Example: a class sings ''Row, row, row your boat'' in unison first. Then the leader splits them into two groups. Group 1 starts, and Group 2 begins when Group 1 reaches ''gently down the stream''. Each group keeps its own beat and listens to the other. At the end, Group 1 finishes first and Group 2 finishes last, so the leader signals the end together.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Listen to my own group and sing a little softer than the others, and look at the leader''s beat. I can also follow the words on a lyric card.'' Staying with your own part and watching the leader helps hold your place.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Believing that singing in a round means you should sing louder than the other group to be heard. Each group sings at the same volume so you can hear both parts. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about unison with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nWhen a group sings together, they can sing in unison, which means everyone sings the same notes at the same time. In a round, such as ''Row, row, row your boat'', the group splits and each part begins at a different time while singing the same tune. A song may have verses that tell the story and a chorus that repeats. To sing together well, listen to the other parts, keep the beat and watch the leader. Count in before starting, and sing at a volume so you can hear the other voices. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 26, Lesson 2: Singing songs together","unit_number":6,"unit_title":"Singing, instruments and composing","week":26,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: a class sings ''Row, row, row your boat'' in unison first. Then the leader splits them into two groups. Group 1 starts, and Group 2 begins when Group 1 reaches ''gently down the stream''. Each group keeps its own beat and listens to the other. At the end, Group 1 finishes first and Group 2 finishes last, so the leader signals the end together.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Unison and a round.","caption":"Same tune, different starts.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Unison</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">everyone sings the same</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Round</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">parts start one after another</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Verse and chorus.","caption":"The chorus repeats.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Verse</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">tells the story</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Chorus</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">repeats each time</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w26-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
